### Summary

`MemoryReuse` currently declines to fuse two tile allocations whenever any field of `TileView` differs — including `valid_shape`. When the underlying storage shape and all other view attributes match, the two tiles could legitimately share a single backing allocation, with each use site projecting its own valid shape via a lightweight op such as `tile.set_validshape` (already available, see closed #991).

Relaxing the compatibility check along these lines would eliminate redundant allocations in unrolled / partially-unrolled kernels without changing semantics.

### Motivation / Use Case

Observed in `qwen3_decode_incore_16` after `PartialUnrollTileLoops` (see `src/ir/transforms/memory_reuse_pass.cpp`, function `AreTileTypesCompatible`). The function body holds two sibling `IfStmt`s over the same `k__idx_v0` chunk loop:

- IfStmt A: activation tile with `valid_shape = [16, pl.min(25600 - k__idx_v0, 64)]`
- IfStmt B: activation tile with `valid_shape = [16, pl.min(25600 - (k__idx_v0 + 64), 64)]`

Physical `shape_` is `[16, 64]` for both, `blayout`, `slayout`, `fractal`, `pad`, `stride`, and `start_offset` all match. Only `valid_shape` differs structurally because each branch offsets `k` differently.

`AreTileTypesCompatible` compares the full `TileView` via `AreTileViewsEqual` (or `TileView::operator==` before the recent `structural_equal` fix) and returns false, so the coloring step cannot reuse. Net result in `incore_16`:

- 4 distinct `mem_left_*` allocations (8, 11, 14, 17) — one per branch — where 1 would cover all uses
- 4 distinct `mem_right_*` allocations (9, 12, 15, 18), same story

The tiles' lifetimes are mutually exclusive (sibling `if/else`), and their backing storage bound is identical, so a single `mem_left` + single `mem_right` would be correct and produce the expected code size.

This pattern will appear in every kernel that combines `PartialUnrollTileLoops` with a tail-chunk boundary guard — which is most chunked matmul/FFN kernels.

### Proposed API / Behavior

Add an opt-in compatibility path in `AreTileTypesCompatible`:

1. Require `dtype_` and `shape_` to match exactly (unchanged).
2. Require all `TileView` fields *except* `valid_shape` to match exactly.
3. When `valid_shape` differs structurally, still consider the tiles compatible for reuse, but record the fact so the lifetime-coloring step can emit a `tile.set_validshape` (or equivalent) at each use site that needs a specific valid shape.

Pseudo-code outline:

```cpp
bool AreTileTypesCompatible(const VarPtr& var1, const VarPtr& var2) {
  auto t1 = As<TileType>(var1->GetType());
  auto t2 = As<TileType>(var2->GetType());
  if (!t1 || !t2) return true;
  if (t1->dtype_ != t2->dtype_) return false;
  if (!AreTileExprVectorsEqual(t1->shape_, t2->shape_)) return false;

  // TileView matching: allow valid_shape to differ, require everything else
  // to match. When they differ, the caller is expected to emit set_validshape
  // at each use site after fusion.
  if (t1->tile_view_.has_value() != t2->tile_view_.has_value()) return false;
  if (t1->tile_view_.has_value()) {
    const auto& a = *t1->tile_view_;
    const auto& b = *t2->tile_view_;
    if (!AreTileExprVectorsEqual(a.stride, b.stride)) return false;
    if (!AreTileExprsEqual(a.start_offset, b.start_offset)) return false;
    if (a.blayout != b.blayout || a.slayout != b.slayout ||
        a.fractal != b.fractal || a.pad != b.pad) return false;
    // valid_shape intentionally not compared.
  }
  return true;
}
```

Then in the reuse-application step, after merging two tiles into one allocation, insert `tile.set_validshape` ops at each use site whose `valid_shape` differs from the representative tile's.

### Alternatives Considered

- **Keep the strict check.** Preserves the current simple model, at the cost of per-branch allocations that inflate `alloc_tile` counts in unrolled kernels. This is the status quo.
- **Canonicalise `valid_shape` earlier.** Teach an earlier pass (e.g. a simplification step right after `PartialUnrollTileLoops`) to rewrite `min(25600 - (k + 64), 64)` into a common form so the two expressions become structurally equal. Fragile — relies on the simplifier recognising every boundary shape pattern — and doesn't generalise to cases where the valid shapes genuinely differ.
- **Always reuse and drop `valid_shape` entirely from reuse checks.** Semantically wrong: codegen legitimately needs per-use valid shape for pad/mask generation.

### Additional Context

- Related closed issue: #991 added the `tile.set_validshape` op, which is the primitive this feature would use.
- Current compatibility check: `src/ir/transforms/memory_reuse_pass.cpp`, function `AreTileTypesCompatible` around line 823.
- The `structural_equal`-based tile comparison is a separate improvement (it fixed a pointer-identity bug where structurally-identical expressions returned unequal); that change is orthogonal to this feature — structural equality makes the strict check work *correctly*, and this feature relaxes it further.

### Summary

Add a new property verifier `AssignTypeSymmetry` that enforces, for every `AssignStmt(var, value)` in any function:

```cpp
structural_equal(var->GetType(), value->GetType())
```

— covering all fields the IR carries on a value's type: `dtype`, `shape`, `memref_`, `memory_space_`, and `tile_view_`. Wire it as a **structural property** so `VerificationInstrument` checks it at pipeline start and before/after every pass.

Related: #1262, #1278 (both are pass-side bugs that produce asymmetric IR — this verifier is the cross-cutting defensive net that would catch them at any stage).

### Motivation / Use Case

PyPTO has no verifier today that checks LHS/RHS type symmetry on `AssignStmt`. Concretely:

- `TypeCheck` (`src/ir/verifier/type_check_pass.cpp`) only overrides `VisitStmt_` for `ForStmt`/`WhileStmt`/`IfStmt`. There is no `VisitStmt_(AssignStmtPtr)`.
- Even where `CheckTypeEquality` runs (on yield/return/condition checks), it does **not** compare `memory_space_` and does not run on assignments.

Result: a pass can mutate `var->type` independently of `value->GetType()` and produce structurally invalid IR that the default verifier silently accepts. #1262 is the canonical example — `InferTileMemorySpace` writes `Mem.Acc` onto a Var whose RHS `tile.full` Call still says `Mem.Vec`. Default verification (`VerificationLevel::BASIC`) does not catch it; the regression test runs with `verification_level=NONE` and asserts only on printed strings, so the asymmetric IR slips through. #1278 documents a related but distinct symptom on the print/parse round-trip path.

A single defensive structural verifier closes this entire class of bug. Per the project's `first-principles.md`: the IR design is the source of truth — `AssignStmt(var, value)` requires `var.type == value.type`, period. Any pass that violates this is buggy by definition; the verifier turns silent corruption into a hard error so we find the bug at the pass that caused it, not three passes later.

### Proposed API / Behavior

#### 1. New verifier — `src/ir/verifier/verify_assign_type_symmetry.cpp`

```cpp
namespace {
class AssignTypeSymmetryVisitor : public IRVisitor {
 public:
  AssignTypeSymmetryVisitor(std::vector<Diagnostic>& diagnostics, std::string func_name)
      : diagnostics_(diagnostics), func_name_(std::move(func_name)) {}

 protected:
  void VisitStmt_(const AssignStmtPtr& op) override {
    if (op && op->var_ && op->value_) {
      auto var_type = op->var_->GetType();
      auto rhs_type = op->value_->GetType();
      if (var_type && rhs_type && !structural_equal(var_type, rhs_type)) {
        std::ostringstream msg;
        msg << "AssignStmt LHS/RHS type mismatch in function '" << func_name_
            << "': var '" << op->var_->name_hint_ << "' has type '"
            << TypeToString(var_type) << "' but RHS has type '"
            << TypeToString(rhs_type) << "'";
        diagnostics_.emplace_back(DiagnosticSeverity::Error,
                                  "AssignTypeSymmetry", /*error_code=*/1,
                                  msg.str(), op->span_);
      }
    }
    IRVisitor::VisitStmt_(op);
  }

 private:
  std::vector<Diagnostic>& diagnostics_;
  std::string func_name_;
};
}  // namespace

class AssignTypeSymmetryPropertyVerifierImpl : public PropertyVerifier {
 public:
  std::string GetName() const override { return "AssignTypeSymmetry"; }
  void Verify(const ProgramPtr& program, std::vector<Diagnostic>& diagnostics) override {
    if (!program) return;
    for (const auto& [gv, func] : program->functions_) {
      if (!func || !func->body_) continue;
      AssignTypeSymmetryVisitor visitor(diagnostics, func->name_);
      visitor.VisitStmt(func->body_);
    }
  }
};

PropertyVerifierPtr CreateAssignTypeSymmetryPropertyVerifier() {
  return std::make_shared<AssignTypeSymmetryPropertyVerifierImpl>();
}
```

Reuse the existing `structural_equal(TypePtr, TypePtr)` from `include/pypto/ir/transforms/structural_comparison.h:78`. For diagnostics with a precise mismatch path, optionally call `assert_structural_equal` and catch the `ValueError` to populate the message.

