### Alternatives Considered

1. **Lattice / "modulo nullopt" mode.** Require equality only when both sides are concrete; treat `nullopt` on either side as compatible. This is what #1262 sketches. Pro: lower bar, no rollout risk. Con: weaker invariant — passes can leave one side `nullopt` indefinitely, which loses information and just defers the same class of bug. Rejected in favor of strict-everywhere because the IR design says `AssignStmt(var, value)` requires symmetric types, period.

2. **Strict-after-stage.** Run the check only after each soft field's responsible pass (`memory_space` after `InferTileMemorySpace`, `memref` after `InitMemRef`, etc.). Pro: matches today's pipeline shape. Con: couples the verifier to pass ordering, and the asymmetry can still propagate downstream silently between the producing pass and the verifying point. Rejected.

3. **Inline `INTERNAL_CHECK` in `AssignStmt` constructor.** Pro: zero extra plumbing. Con: prevents passes from constructing transient asymmetric IR even when they are about to fix it; the project pattern for IR-shape invariants is verifier passes (see `TileTypeCoherence`), not constructor checks. Rejected.

### Additional Context

#### Roll-out plan (important — strict-everywhere will surface latent violations)

Promoting straight to structural will break existing tests because passes like the one in #1262 already produce asymmetric IR. Two-step rollout:

1. **Land disabled.** Implement the verifier and enum value, register the factory, but **do not** add it to `GetStructuralProperties()`. Instead add it to `GetDefaultVerifyProperties()` (`src/ir/transforms/ir_property.cpp:151`) so it runs at `VerificationLevel::Full`. This surfaces every violation in CI / dev runs without breaking the default pipeline.
2. **Triage and fix.** Each violation becomes its own bug (#1262-style). Once the test suite is clean against the verifier in Full mode, promote to `GetStructuralProperties()` and update `99-verifier.md`.

#### Open caveats

- **TupleType / multi-return AssignStmts.** `AssignStmt` carries a single `var_` and `value_`, so tuple-typed RHS would need both sides to share the `TupleType`. Need to confirm whether the parser/SSA passes ever produce a single AssignStmt with TupleType, or whether they always destructure. `structural_equal` already handles `TupleType` via reflection, so behaviorally this should "just work", but worth a deliberate test case during implementation.
- **`assert_structural_equal` for diagnostics.** The header at `include/pypto/ir/transforms/structural_comparison.h:106` advertises a detailed-diff `ValueError` message. Catching that and forwarding into a `Diagnostic` gives much better error messages than a hand-rolled `TypeToString` comparison; worth doing in the same change.
- **Relation to existing rules.** `TileTypeCoherence` already checks the canonical-encoding invariant on individual `TileType` instances. This proposal complements it by checking pair-wise equality across the LHS/RHS of an assignment, which is orthogonal — a pair of TileTypes can each be canonical yet still be unequal.

#### Why issue #1262 stays open

#1262 tracks the **`InferTileMemorySpace` pass bug**, where the pass produces asymmetric IR for fixed-memory producers. This verifier *detects* such bugs at any stage but does not fix them — `InferTileMemorySpace` still has to either insert an explicit `tile.move` or refuse to back-propagate onto fixed-memory producers (per #1262's "Expected IR" section). The verifier landing in disabled mode (Phase 1 above) is what will demonstrate that #1262 still violates the rule, so the two issues are complementary, not duplicates.

## Summary

When an `@pl.function(type=InCore, attrs={\"split\": ...})` kernel has **multiple `pl.Out[...]` parameters but a single (non-tuple) return**, the orchestration codegen incorrectly aliases the result SSA to the *first* Out parameter instead of the one actually returned. Calling such a kernel via `with pl.spmd(N): out = self.kernel(...)` produces wrong downstream consumers — they read scratch data instead of the real output.

## Reproducer (pattern)

```python
@pl.function(type=pl.FunctionType.InCore, attrs={\"split\": pl.SplitMode.UP_DOWN})
def fa_fused_kernel(
    self,
    q: pl.Tensor[[..., ...], pl.BF16],
    all_raw_scores: pl.Out[pl.Tensor[[..., ...], pl.FP32]],  # scratch (1st Out)
    all_exp_padded: pl.Out[pl.Tensor[[..., ...], pl.BF16]], # scratch
    # ... more scratch ...
    attn_out:        pl.Out[pl.Tensor[[..., ...], pl.BF16]],  # actual output (last Out)
) -> pl.Tensor[[..., ...], pl.BF16]:
    # ... mutate scratch via pl.assemble, accumulate into attn_out ...
    return attn_out   # single-Var return

@pl.function(type=pl.FunctionType.Opaque)
def main(self, ...):
    ...
    with pl.spmd(N, name_hint=\"fa_fused\"):
        attn_out = self.fa_fused_kernel(q, all_raw_scores, ..., attn_out)
    # subsequent consumers of `attn_out` end up reading `all_raw_scores`
```

## Generated orchestration C++ (the bug)

```cpp
params_t6.add_input(...);
params_t6.add_output(all_raw_scores_gm);   // 1st Out
params_t6.add_output(all_exp_padded_gm);
params_t6.add_output(all_cur_mi_gm);
params_t6.add_output(all_cur_li_gm);
params_t6.add_output(all_oi_tmp_gm);
params_t6.add_output(attn_out);            // last Out — the one returned
MixedKernels mixed_6 = {6, 7, 7};
params_t6.launch_spec.set_block_num(128);
rt_submit_task(mixed_6, params_t6);
const Tensor& attn_out__ssa_v1 = all_raw_scores_gm;   //  BUG: aliased to 1st Out, not attn_out
```

Downstream tasks consume `attn_out__ssa_v1`, so they pick up the QK matmul scratch buffer (`all_raw_scores_gm`) instead of the final attention output. Numerically the consumers see NaN or large garbage values (depending on whether the scratch tensor was zero-initialised).

## Root cause

`src/codegen/orchestration/orchestration_codegen.cpp::GenerateSingleReturnAlias`:

```cpp
void GenerateSingleReturnAlias(const CallPtr& call, const std::string& var_name) {
  FunctionPtr callee = program_->GetFunction(call->op_->name_);
  if (!callee) return;
  auto out_indices = CollectOutIndices(callee);
  if (!out_indices.empty()) {
    EmitTensorAlias(var_name, call, out_indices[0]);   //  always aliases to FIRST Out
  }
}
```

The single-return path unconditionally picks `out_indices[0]`, ignoring which Out parameter the callee actually returns. The corresponding lineage walker at the top of the function (lines ~467–488) makes the same assumption (\"the result aliases the FIRST output-side arg\").

This is safe when there is exactly one Out parameter (the common case in current model kernels), but breaks as soon as a kernel carries scratch tensors as additional Outs and returns only the real output.

## Suggested fix

Trace the function's `ReturnStmt` value back to a `Param` of `callee`, then alias against the call-arg at that param's index. Walk `AssignStmt` chains to follow SSA renaming (e.g. `attn_out = pl.assemble(attn_out, ...)` re-binds the Var name) until a `Param` is hit. Fall back to `out_indices[0]` only if no return-to-Param mapping is found.

The lineage walker around line 467 needs the same treatment so the alias-propagation graph doesn't pull non-returned Outs into the result's alias class.

A regression test in `tests/ut/codegen/test_orchestration_codegen.py` should mirror `test_spmd_mixed_program` with **multiple Out params + single return**, then assert that the emitted alias targets the returned Out (not the first one).

## Impact

Blocks using `pl.spmd` over mixed (split=UP_DOWN) kernels that carry per-block GM scratch as additional `pl.Out` params — a useful pattern for batched flash-attention-style kernels that need per-block scratch but only want to expose the final accumulator to downstream tasks.

