## Summary

This PR adds an opt-in, entirely in-tree `MemoryPlanner.DSA_RP` that considers both memory capacity and the performance cost of physical buffer reuse.

The motivation is simple: two buffers may have disjoint logical lifetimes and therefore be legal to overlap, while that overlap still creates an artificial handoff between asynchronous execution resources. A footprint-only allocator cannot distinguish cheap reuse from reuse that delays execution.

Initial A2/A3 results are promising:

- Up to **28.7% standalone-kernel speedup**.
- **13 confirmed kernel wins across 6 source programs**.
- **0 confirmed regressions among 60 timed kernels** in the large-kernel sweep.
- Approximately **1.0–1.6% end-to-end improvement** on selected Qwen3 prefill and decode programs in focused studies.
- No device-correctness regression found in the completed validation campaigns.

All tested placements were generated by the PyPTO planner. They were not manually placed, replayed from an offline optimizer, or selected using profiling feedback.

`MemoryPlanner.PYPTO` remains the default. DSA-RP is opt-in while evaluation and cost-model research continue.

