## Performance methodology

Standalone measurements use paired planner comparisons:

```text
PYPTO -> DSA-RP -> DSA-RP -> PYPTO
```

Correctness and comparability are checked before timing:

- identical kernel ABI;
- identical logical buffer spans;
- deterministic outputs within each planner;
- bit-identical outputs across planners;
- normalized pre-synchronization PTO differing only in placement addresses.

Candidate effects are confirmed on a second device with reversed order. Device-dependent or statistically unresolved cases are not counted as wins.

The large-kernel sweep compared 298 placement endpoints. Sixty standalone kernels passed input recovery, correctness, and timing:

- 13 confirmed beneficial;
- 0 confirmed regressions;
- 5 device-dependent;
- 3 not confirmed;
- 39 below the screening threshold.

A further 235 candidates were not treated as null results: their standalone inputs or scalars could not be recovered safely, so the harness refused to guess them.

## Important observations

The experimental work produced several useful constraints on the model:

- Synchronization-event count is not a reliable latency objective.
- A placement may be faster while PTOAS emits more synchronization operations.
- The dynamic location of a handoff matters more than its static pipe-pair label alone.
- Reuse on a frequently executed loop-carried path can be expensive.
- Multiple buffer-pair hazards may collapse into a single consumer-side release frontier.
- Some legal overlaps are neutral because an existing dependency already gates the consumer.
- Apparent single-device effects frequently disappear or reverse on another device.

The product recognizer therefore stays deliberately simple and non-negative. Richer consumer-aware aggregation, dynamic-frequency weights, and completion-frontier modeling remain research topics.

## Ongoing research project

This PR is the first production-oriented checkpoint of a broader, ongoing DSA-RP research project.

The standalone [`tonibohnlein/dsa-solver`](https://github.com/tonibohnlein/dsa-solver) repository contains the research framework:

- portable DSA and DSA-RP problem representations;
- independent validation and objective recomputation;
- compiler-derived PyPTO and PyPTO-Lib corpora;
- MiniMalloc-derived benchmark instances;
- canonical greedy;
- [placement-aware DSA-RP local search](https://github.com/tonibohnlein/dsa-solver/blob/main/src/dsa/algorithms/reuse_penalty_local_search_solver.cpp);
- exact and special-case solvers;
- an exact-solver portfolio;
- TVM-style hill climbing;
- XLA-style heap placement;
- Cypress-style relaxation and other comparison baselines.

The current algorithms and their applicability are documented in the [DSA-RP algorithm notes](https://github.com/tonibohnlein/dsa-solver/blob/main/docs/dsa_rp_algorithms.md).

The standalone repository is intentionally the research vehicle for:

- evaluating alternative algorithms;
- studying harder benchmark instances;
- comparing against exact or best-known objectives;
- refining penalty-edge construction;
- testing local-search neighborhoods;
- determining whether a more expensive solver materially improves over canonical greedy.

Mature findings can later be ported into PyPTO. This PR does not introduce the external repository as a dependency.

The design and research status are tracked in [issue #1980](https://github.com/hw-native-sys/pypto/issues/1980).

## Scope and non-goals

This PR includes:

- the opt-in DSA-RP planner;
- mechanical penalty recognition;
- the in-tree canonical-greedy solver;
- independent validation;
- planner configuration and bindings;
- host and system-test coverage;
- required operator access annotations.

It intentionally does not include:

- the standalone research solver as a dependency;
- placement export or replay;
- device profiling infrastructure;
- hand-authored placements;
- fitted per-kernel weights;
- negative reuse rewards;
- PTOAS synchronization simulation;
- GraphSyncSolver integration;
- a claim that synchronization count predicts latency;
- a claim that the current recognizer is the final cost model.

